> E-mail: sergio@scisun.sci.ccny.cuny.edu

It is HIGHLY unlikely that changing the IP addresses caused your
problem, but rather uncovered an already existing one. here are a few
cases:

1. has the network traffic on the machines increased/does the telnet
daemon run constantly at 99% and consume large amounts of real memory?
2. was the network reconfingured in such a way that network traffic
"passes through" these systems; i.e. do you have users/processes getting
to the alphas just to get somewhere else?
3. does the program in question rely on network access to do part of its
job? if so, the process may be having access problems, which is manifest
as the prolonged run time

Please note that this is NOT a completely exhuastive list of network
related problems, just what I could immediate think of!

If you have not already, check the date on the executable program file
to see if it has changed recently; also check any other programs, shell
scripts, and data files used by the program to ensure that the problem
is not with one of them. Hope this helps.
