Dr. Strangelove wrote:
> 
> On 22 Oct 1997 20:54:16 GMT, nsd@bbc.com (Nick Duffek) wrote:
> 
> >A customer has been experiencing hangs on VT320 and VT420 terminals connected
> >to a Digital UNIX 4.0B Alpha system via a DECserver 100.
> >
> >The exact symptom is that key presses cease propagating to the application.
> >When this happens, it is possible to write data to the terminal, e.g. by
> >running "cat /etc/inittab > /dev/hung-term", but it seems impossible to read
> >data from the terminal, e.g. by running "cat /dev/hung-term".
> 
> We had a problem which sounds very similar to this using LAT and DEC
> Server 90TL's, unfortunately DEC have not been able to provide us with
> an adequate reason or solution for the behaviour. We resolved it by
> telneting to the Alpha from the DEC Server instead.

Ah, we had that too, and reported it. It was never resolved to our
satisfaction.

The problem, as they said, was hardware. The terminals were connected
using only three wires - no control lines, hence no hardware flow
control. If the terminal was turned off while a session was connected,
you got the above effect. Disconnecting the sessions released the TTY.

Digital said to wire them with true RS-232 serial cables. Unfortunately,
the DECserver 100 only _had_ three pins on the connectors - it wasn't a
cable issue.

We trased the DECservers anyways, and replaced the terminals with
Ethernet-connected PCs.

