Adnan Yaqub wrote:
> 
> The System: DEC Alpha Server 2100, Digital Unix 4.0a, DNSES EISA Sync Card.
> 
> The Question: How do I get Unix to see the card?
> 
> What I have done:
> 
> 1) Installed card in EISA bus.
> 2) Ran ECU to set up the card.
> 3) Rebooted.
> 4) Installed DECs WAN stuff.
> 

Adnan,

If the SRM console doesn't quibble about a misconfigured EISA bus during
boot, then what you've done (above) is correct.  But, it's just a start.

Getting a device driver installed in the OS is much more complex.  If
this is DEC's card (or another vendor), then they should have given you
an installation script to install the device.  There is no wrote
procedure that does not require knowledge of the driver software.

If this is your device (or your brave enough to try to configure a
anothers device), then you need the "Writting Device Drivers: Tutorial",
DEC part number AA-PUBVD-TE.  This explains how to install device
drivers.  You have two choices: dynamically and statically configured. 
A dynamically configured device driver is much easier to install (and
remove) but requires more code in the device driver.  A statically
configured device driver (which requires modification the files you
mentioned) is much trickier on the user end, but requires less code in
the driver itself (is easier to program).  

If you're going to change the files you mentioned, you really need a
copy of the manual.  Chapters 13 & 14 cover the installation.

Chris
