OK, gang ...  Here's one I've never seen before, even though we
have many different types of SCSI drives, attached to dozens of
workstations (DEC, Sun, SGI, and various others).  I haven't got
a clue as to whether this is a Seagate (mis)feature, or some bug
(or design silliness) in Digital UNIX, but here's the problem:

We recently installed a Seagate ST42351W (the 23 GByte Elite) on
a DEC Personal Workstation (a 433au, running Digital UNIX 4.0c).
When the disk hasn't been accessed within some time window (I've
not been able to quantify how long it has to be idle), it spins
down!  This is *really* annoying, because the next time the disk
is accessed, there's a long (~30 second) delay, while the drive
spins up again.

I can think of two possible culprits here:

(1)  The drive itself might be spinning itself down if it isn't
accessed for some time period.  This seems highly unlikely.  As
far as I can determine, there's nothing in anything that Seagate
supplied (or on their web page) that even hints at this sort of
behaviour.

(2)  Digital's SCSI driver might be sending a "stop" command to
the disk if there is no access within the time window.  But this
seems unlikely as well:  Why on earth should it?  And again, one
can't find anything in any of Digital's documentation that talks
about drive spindown by the SCSI driver.

But it has to be one of those two.  And (unless one of those two
vendors fesses up) how can one even tell, short of putting some
sort of SCSI analyzer on the bus, to see whether the DEC box is
really sending a "stop" command to tell the drive to spin down?

