Glen Turner wrote:
> > ... gcc doesn't know anything worth mentioning about instruction
> > scheduling or memory-wise behaviour.
> 
> Actually, gcc is one of the better compilers in this respect.

Well, that's a bit of a stretch :-)

gcc 2.7.* does no serious alias analysis or static branch prediction, and
it's instruction scheduling support is pretty weak (basic block scheduling
only, and no software pipelining). gcc has a pretty good global register
allocator, and reasonable global optimizations (strength reduction etc), but
that's about it.

> It's just that the Alpha processor support is quick and nasty.

That is also true.

> The Alpha Linux project is aware of the need to reimplement
> the gcc support for Alpha in a more complete way, and are
> encouraging people within DEC to do so.

Actually, the egcs project ( http://www.cygnus.com/egcs/ ) is doing several
things to improve gcc's optimization abilities. It is adding alias analysis
and global instruction scheduling, among other things. egcs is still very
much in beta testing though.

But at the moment, gcc is no match for the vendors compilers on Alpha 21164,
UltraSPARC or MIPS R10000, and even on older/narrower processors (Alpha
21064, Super/hyperSPARC, MIPS R4x00) gcc is at least 10-20% off the pace on
SPEC and similar benchmarks.

On the other hand, gcc is infinitely cheaper than the vendors compilers :-)


