Hugues Talbot wrote:
>         We use Gcc and DEC C a fair bit around here on 21064
> processors.  In our experience, it is not true that DEC C is
> significantly faster than gcc.  At most 10-20%

Yes, but the 21064 is only a dual issue processor, so the weaknesses in gcc's
instruction scheduler are a lot less important. On the 4 issue 21164 the
difference is much more noticeable.

> The DEC FORTRAN compiler is nothing short of amazing in terms of
> the optimization that it can perform. This is apparently due to the
> structure of the language, which is generally considered easier to
> optimize (but I'm not a specialist of these questions) than C.

FORTRAN has rules which prohibit certain classes of aliasing between function
arguments. So for a FORTRAN function taking two arrays as arguments, the
compiler can safely assume that the two arrays don't overlap, whereas this is
not true in C or C++. Similarly, FORTRAN (77) doesn't really have the concept
of pointers.

Both these things make alias analysis and dependency checking much easier, so
that more things can be done in parallel. This really goes to the extreme on
vector supercomputers (eg Cray), where finding this parallelism is critical:
array addition is a single instruction on a Cray if you can prove that the
arrays don't overlap.

> A factor of more than 30% between DEC C and gcc is hardly believable
> on a complex application.

For most C programs, probably yes, because C programs tend to be more memory
bound (pointer chasing etc). But for inner loops which get software pipelined,
a factor of 2 is quite possible (especially for FORTRAN, but even for C programs).


JASON PATTERSON
