X-Deja-AN: 281414723
References: <34462CF3.41C6@whoi.edu>
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


eddie scheer (escheer@whoi.edu) wrote:
: I did a plain installation of gcc and g77 on my Alpha box runnix
: DEC UNIX. Programs seem to be about 10x slower than those compiled with
: DEC Fortran. Is this to be expected?

Yes, it is. Cause gcc doesn't know anything worth mentioning about instruction
scheduling or memory-wise behaviour. The factor of 10 is heavy, maybe it's a
benchmarking problem, but a factor of >> 2 is realistic.

This one of the reasons why DEC's Linux/Alpha project does not come up to pace,
the Alpha could go much faster, but it would need the right compiler technology.
Still it is incredibly fast.

My suggestion: do use gcc for development work such as syntax checking, and
DEC C for the final production compilation.

