eddie scheer wrote:
> I did a plain installation of gcc and g77 on my Alpha box runnix
> DEC UNIX. Programs seem to be about 10x slower than those compiled with
> DEC Fortran. Is this to be expected?

10x is probably an exaggeration, right?

But yes, the GNU compilers have a lot less powerful optimizers, particularly
when it comes to instruction scheduling, which is a key performance factor on
the Alpha's and UltraSPARC's, unlike the Pentium Pro, MIPS R10000 or PowerPC
604 all of which do a fair bit of dynamic scheduling. The compiler really has
to "spoon feed" the pipelines of the Alpha 21164 and UltraSPARC to get
maximum performance, and the relatively poor scheduler in gcc/g++/g77 simply
doesn't do that very well.

A factor of 2 difference between g77 and DEC's compiler is quite reasonable.
Anything more than that is probably a sign of pathological cache behavior
(which might also be a compiler issue of a different type :-)


