In article <34396F50.41C6@gsfc.nasa.gov> David Alban <david.alban@gsfc.nasa.gov> writes:

> In some unixes the file /etc/issue is displayed after telnet and ftp
> connections are made but before the user logs in.  I have an Alpha
> running "OSF1 V3.2".  I have created an /etc/issue file, but it seems to
> be ignored.  Can anyone tell me how to display a message (you know like,
> "If you're not supposed to log in, and you do, you'll begin to itch
> uncontrollably" :-) to users who have connected and are about to log in
> or ftp in?

Well, I checked and found the following:

man issue
  - works on terminals spawned from /etc/inittab, read by getty daemon
    (usually LAT terminals, not telnet connections)

man issue.net
  - works on terminals opened by telnetd, read by telnetd

man gettydefs
  - a message < 79 characters can be output to a defined terminal

man telnetd
  - quote from man page:

 "By default, the telnetd daemon starts the login dialog using the login
  string specified in the message field of the /etc/gettydefs file.  If an
  /etc/issue.net file present, telnetd reads the file and writes its contents
  over a new telnet connection prior to starting the login dialog.  If an
  /etc/issue file is present, but not a /etc/issue.net file, telnetd reads
  that file and writes its contents over a new telnet connection instead."

The last line appears to be in error, as it conflicts with the descriptions
from the issue(4) and issue.net(4) name pages.

man ftpd
  - no information found on setting messages for the ftp service



To sum up - change the file name from issue to issue.net, and telnet
should get a message now.


