In article <343AAD60.6DC5FEC0@pp.kolumbus.fi> Juhani Jaakola <juhani.jaakola@pp.kolumbus.fi> writes:
>We have an AlphaServer 4100 with Digital UNIX 3.2 running an Oracle
>database. The database is now 25 GB but is expected to grow to 80 GB. I
>need to backup those 80 GB of Oracle data files in no more than 6 hours.

80 Gbyte in 6 hours means you need to sustain better than 3.7 Mbyte/sec. .
So this looks like a job for a TZ89 (which can do this uncompressed), or
if you believe that your data compresses, a TZS20 may be a more economical
choice. Of course both devices cannot store a full 80 Gbytes on a single
cartridge (the TZ89 is a DLT7000 with 35 Gbyte native capacity), the TZS20
is a Sony AIT drive with 25 Gbyte native capacity. Add a compression
factor of your choice on top of that and you see how many tape changes
you need. The AIT drive is 3 MByte/sec. native so you would need
some healthy compression to get more than 3.7 MByte/sec. sustained
(remember that you need to change tapes in mid-air, costing time
and hence bandwidth).

We use DLT's.

