>Unable to bind socket -- is another agent running?
			.
			.
			.
I think you can ignore the "is another agent running" question;
the boot procedure is confused by that time, and is being more
polite than helpful.  It sounds very much like what it most ap-
pears on the surface to be:  your boot procedure doesn't have
security privileges (permission) to access the SNMP port (161).
Do this:  after boot-up, su to root, then (attempt to) launch
(/usr/sbin/)snmpd.  If it starts up, the evidence is that your
start-ups have been mangled.

If you feel safe passing on an IP address, and aren't firewall-
ing, I can interrogate your agent to confirm its operation.
Depending on your commitment to the topic, you can also learn
enough SNMP to do so within your network.
