Dylan Salisbury wrote:> 
> After a link error during a make, I end up with the partially linked
> executable remaining!  It's my understanding that make should always
> delete a failed target unless the .PRECIOUS special target is specified,
> correct?
> 
> Are there any flags or commands I can use to get around this problem
> without switching development tools?  If not, I'd love recommendations
> for the next-least-painful solution (which I imagine is to go GNU?)

I've never heard of a make rule that it delete a failed target but
I'm no expert on make.

What I _have_ seen is lots of makefiles that look like this:

foo: $(foo_OBJS)
     cc -o $@.X $(foo_OBJS)
     mv $@.X $@

I.e. they link into a temporary target and then move it to real target.
If the link fails, the 'mv' won't be executed.


