 Juhani Jaakola <juhani.jaakola@pp.kolumbus.fi> writes:
>We have an AlphaServer 4100 with Digital UNIX 3.2 running an Oracle
>database. The database is now 25 GB but is expected to grow to 80 GB. I
>need to backup those 80 GB of Oracle data files in no more than 6 hours.
>
>What device/software is most economical way to do this?
>
>I don't need any fancy features, cpio/tar and a tape drive without any
>jukebox features is enough. The only requirement is performance as
>stated above.

As mentioned several times, you should probably look at using a DLT, either
purchased from DEC or from Quantum.  We are using the older TZ87 which only
hold 20 GB, but are very happy with them.

I have no knowledge of how Oracle stores its data on disk, (does it use raw
devices or does it uses "files" in a filesystems ?) or how Oracle does
"backups".  However, for backing up regular files in a filesystem, the
Polycenter Network Save and Restore (NSR) that DEC provides as "standard
equipment" is certainly more than capable of doing the job.  One of the
"nice" features of NSR is that it handles multiple tape drive, something I
would strongly recommend for your application (at least 2, better yet 4). 

Since V4.2 of NSR, there is now the capability of a "call out" before a
filesystem is backed up.  This allows you to write your own script to
"lock" application files before they are backed up.

NSR is actually a re-packaged version of Legatto's backup software. 
Legatto sells an "add-on" for backing up Oracle databases.

Jack Patteeuw

