On Wed, 08 Oct 1997 00:45:04 +0300, Juhani Jaakola
<juhani.jaakola@pp.kolumbus.fi> wrote in article
<343AAD60.6DC5FEC0@pp.kolumbus.fi>:

>We have an AlphaServer 4100 with Digital UNIX 3.2 running an Oracle
>database. The database is now 25 GB but is expected to grow to 80 GB. I
>need to backup those 80 GB of Oracle data files in no more than 6 hours.
>
>What device/software is most economical way to do this?

I don't know how practical this is, either on D.UNIX or in your
environment. But, if you mirror the disks (is it LSM on DUNIX?) during
normal operation, what you could do is shut the system down (circa few
minutes), and bring the system up again, but running on only one half of
the mirror (circa a few minutes). Back up that mirror at your leisure,
whilst your system keeps running with only a few minutes downtime. When the
backup is complete (hopefully less than 23 hours, if it's a daily backup!)
you can re-merge the mirror sets - IIRC you can do this on-line with LSM. 

Just make sure you split the mirror when the system is down to ensure a
consistent mirror set.

Alternatively, get in a decent Oracle contractor who should be able to tell
you how to back up the database whilst it's running. Something to do with
the redo logs, but then I'm not an Oracle expert.

Greg



