>
> We have an AlphaServer 4100 with Digital UNIX 3.2 running an Oracle
> database. The database is now 25 GB but is expected to grow to 80 GB. I
> need to backup those 80 GB of Oracle data files in no more than 6 hours.
>
> What device/software is most economical way to do this?
>
> I don't need any fancy features, cpio/tar and a tape drive without any
> jukebox features is enough. The only requirement is performance as
> stated above.

I just finished writing some ksh scripts to handle Oracle backups.  As
has been mentioned several times, you want a DLT to handle the
throughput, and probably a loader to handle auto-loading multiple tapes
as required.  To minimize downtime, you probably also want to use
Oracle's ARCHIVELOGMODE to enable Hot backups of your tables.  However,
you still need to do Cold backups occasionally so that you can clean up
the archive log files.

Since Oracle uses files in the regular filesystem, if you are using DEC's
ADVFS filesystem you can also use the 'clonefset' command to create a
read-only "copy" of the filesystem and take your backup from the clone. 
It only takes a few seconds to create a clone, and once the clone has
been created you can bring the database back online, minimizing downtime.
 Check out http://apache.ethz.ch/docu/AFA402/advfs_admin_guide.html for
info on ADVFS and cloning.

Hope this helps.
