Ron Foerch <foerch@nwnumerics.com> writes:

>>         To keep our SGIs happy, we button the Digital Unix boxes down
>> to NFS version 2.  As of 5.3, SGI had trouble with the 64bit pointers
>> from the DEC machines; this is probably not true of 6.4, but I haven't
>> tried it yet.
>> 
>hmm.. this comment seems like it may have some relevence to 
>a problem that i have with my digital unix machine 
>(OSF1 marmotte V3.2 41 alpha) & it's nfs connection.  the 
>performence is *so* bad. i've been told it might be the 64 bit
>words being passed... another guy told me slow nfs was 
>probably some feature dec though up...

No, between 175 Mhz Alphas I see 9 MByte/sec NFS V3 over UDP and FDDI
to a memory file system (slower than a ramdisk, but MFS can page to
swap space).  Those systems are no longer available, current low end
has much faster CPU.  NFS over TCP is 8 MB/sec, something I suspect
current CPUs would help.

What 64 bit pointers?  If your file sizes are less than 2 GB, you
won't have any trouble with file offsets.  We don't do funny things
with directory cookies, but SGI shouldn't have any trouble if we did
since XFS offers funny cookies.  File data is just a byte stream.  All
this sounds like a smoke screen.  If you have performance data, I'd
like to see it.  The last data I saw was posted to USENET more than 2
years ago and is pretty obsolete.

>anyway.. are there any quick fixes to try?

Buy Alphas.  If you have to use NFS V2, keep in mind that DEC won't
offer async write support, but our write gathering techniques help a
lot.  (The safe async writes of V3 work just fine.)  More biods on the
client and maybe more nfsds on the server can help V2 but aren't as
important for V3.

