> What do people recommend for this config? Making the /dev/re1g partition
> an LSM volume and then putting one Advfs domain on it? Breaking the
> re1g volume into smaller chunks and then using LSM with AdvFS on top?
> Just giving the re1g partition to Advfs as a domain without using LSM?
> Using LSM to break the re1g partition into smaller chunks and then putting
> the chunks into the AdvFS domain? Etc. etc. etc.
> 
> Any help is much appreciated.
> 
> - Justus Addiss

First of all, the only function of LSM is (beside replacing LVM) a
software RAID 0 and 1. If you already have RAID controller (looks that
way) then you really do not need LSM. It would be just an additional
complication for you and the machine.

When using AdvFS, you can make a file-domain from multiple partitions,
but it's better to use one partition, if possible. If you want to gain
speed by paralelizing disk I/O, have in mind two things:
1. It's complicated and requires a lot of work, prior and after,
2. It's better to use hardware acceleration like RAID.
 You can always create multiple file-sets in asingle domain. Keep the
structure as simple as possible. Trying to combine several partitions in
a domain in order to get more speed falls down in your case, since you
have hardware RAID 5. So, don't make complex creations, you don't have
to.

That's about it.

Cheers,
Nix.



