In article <34529e3a.104178220@alice.jcc.com> jeff@jcc.com (Jeffrey Jalbert) writes:

> It seems that we are a target of unauthorized SPAM authors.  We think that
> they are using our SMTP port to relay mail through our system. [We have
> recieved a number of complaints from some patient people sharing the mail
> with us.]

> We are currently running Digital Unix version 3.2C. [An upgrade is
> scheduled...]  Digital has indicated that they do not have a fix for this.

Correct as stated. This is not a 'bug' in the traditional sense, so would
not be patched/fixed by Digital. It is more of a configuration issue.

> Looking at the descriptions for SENDMAIL version 8.8, this has just what we
> want to be able to suppress the unauthorized mail, in addition to kill
> filters right at the SENDMAIL interface.  Everything we want [at least
> for the moment!]

Yup. I've read it too. Sounds great. You can also compile in support
from TCP wrappers, which also allows spam control.

> We certainly are not SENDMAIL gurus.  Has anyone out there tried this
> version on DU?  Is there a compiled version somewhere?

Like I said, it is a config file issue, not a compilation issue. Check
out some of the web pages dedicated to dealing with spammers. A few from
my bookmark file:
   http://www.sendmail.org/antispam.html
   http://www.glenns.org/sendmail.antispam.html
   http://www.panix.com/e-spam.html

> It seems to me that the Digital response is remarkably uncooperative.  How
> does one start a campaign to get them to include this basic kind of
> functionality in their product?

That is a complicated issue. As I said, the functionality is already there,
it just isn't activated by default. That is left up to the individual user
to investigate and use if desired.

"Why can't Digital just activate it and preload it for me?" some have asked
in the past. Here's some reasons why not.

1) Necessity

Many systems don't need this protection - some bastion host takes care of
it, or the firewall blocks it, or they aren't connected to the Internet,
or they have other methods of blocking/sorting out the chaff, or ...

2) Speed

A large set of blocked hosts can slow the processing of messages; for large
sites, this can be a real drawback. Since the sites of attack change daily,
the lists grow too fast for anyone to keep up.

3) Politics

The biggie - who to block, and what are the consequences? Say Digital decides
to set the filter to block all mail from the Spamco Corporation, a company
known for sending out spam mail. Even if 100% of sites using mail filters
said "I block mail from Spamco", for Digital (or any vendor) to say "We'll
configure that block for you" would be suicide. It would open them up to
lawsuits, spam attacks directed specifically at them (although they could
countersue then), boycotts, etc. That's why you don't see ANY vendors doing
this, and why websites on how to deal with it proliferate.


My advice - check out the websites, maybe pick up a copy of the O'Reilly
Sendmail book (well worth it IMHO), and set up some blocks based on YOUR
needs.

MarkG

Note: I work for Digital, but I certainly don't speak for them. All
thoughts expressed are mine alone.
