References: <3420AB59.167E@resptk.bhp.com.au>
Reply-To: simons@zk3.dec.com
To: Joe Spanicek <joe@resptk.bhp.com.au>
Organization: DIGITAL UNIX  Development Environment
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


Joe Spanicek wrote:
> If a call to 'realloc' fails and returns a NULL to indicate it has,
> what actually happens to the original memory block passed as a pointer
> reference into the function ??

When a call to realloc fails and returns NULL, the original has not been
modified.  It ia available to the application to continue processing as
the application sees fit.

Some caveats:

When the application calls realloc(old, 0) this is considered a synonym
for free(old).  realloc() returns NULL, and the old memory is free.

When free is called on a chunk of memory 632 bytes or smaller, it is
placed in a special way station for one additional free operation. 
While it is in the waystation, it can be successfully realloc'd.  THIS
IS NOT SUGGESTED PRACTICE!  This is a hack to accommodate a large class
of buggy programs.  There is no waystation for blocks above 632 bytes in
size.  Once freed, they have had portions of the block overwritten with
bookkeeping information.  In the first case (<= 632 bytes), the realloc
can succeed.  In the latter case (>632 bytes) the realloc will fail, the
memory pointed to by your pointer has not been touched by the realloc
operation, but it HAS ALREADY BEEN MODIFIED by the free operation.

Al Simons
DIGITAL UNIX Development Environment
simons@zk3.dec.com



