Subject: dreadful problem with cc + IRIX NFS client + DEC NFS server
Date: 1997/10/01
Message-ID: <EHDoL5.65s@utcc.utoronto.ca>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 277056835
Sender: nntp@utcc.utoronto.ca (News)
Reply-To: mark@cita.utoronto.ca
X-Nntp-Posting-Host: rorqual.cita.utoronto.ca
Organization: Canadian Institute for Theoretical Astrophysics
Newsgroups: comp.sys.sgi.admin,comp.sys.sgi.bugs,comp.unix.osf.osf1


I arrived at 4:30 this morning, intending to get some real work
done, but unfortunately had to deal with the fact that we were
suffering from a self-inflicted denial-of-service attack!  Thus
far, I don't know whether the problem is with SGI's NFS client
software, or Digital's NFS server software, or something else.
But I do know what state things were in.  Maybe someone will be
able to point me to an appropriate {SGI|DEC} patch.

Late last night, someone started a make going on our Origin 2000.
It hung, so he started it again.  It hung, too, so he started it
a third time.  Then he gave up and went to sleep.  When I arrived
this morning, I found ...

  --  Six copies of /usr/lib32/cmplrs/be running on our
      Origin 2000, each eating CPU time like crazy; they
      were unkillable, even with "kill -9".

  --  A totally hosed DEC Alpha (running DUnix 3.2c); it
      would respond to pings, and if I typed a RETURN at
      it from an already-logged-in window on my machine,
      I might get an echo and another shell prompt a few
      minutes later.  But that's about all it would do.

A bit more investigation (luckily, we have ethernet switches that
can tell us things like this) uncovered the fact that the SGI box
was spewing out about 4000 packets per second, about half of which
were being sent to the DEC machine.  (No idea where the other half
ended up.)  This seemed to be the reason that the DUnix system was
so badly hosed.  Since the compiler back-end processes on the O2000
were unkillable, I just rebooted it, and everything settled down to
normal.

(The DEC machine got rebooted as well, before I realized that the
packet flood was responsible for its nearly-moribund state.)

After everything was back up, the directory on the DEC machine in
which the O2000 was working had six .nfs* files (one left behind
be each of the "be" processes, presumably), as well as a directory
named tp.ipaa000Na, whose name and contents seem to be related to
the make target that was being worked on ...

tp: tp.o dbhforce.o appdisk.o query.o
	$(LD) $(CFLAGS) tp.o dbhforce.o appdisk.o query.o \
	-o tp $(LIBS)

... and the contents of which included four object files (the ones
named in the first line of the above makefile excerpt), and a pair
of .I files (tp.I and dbhforce.I), which "file" informs me are ELF
N32 intermediate object files.  One of the .o files (dbhforce.o) is
empty.  There's also something called "objectlist", which contains
a bunch of compiler options, names of .o files, names of .so files,
and the like.

I presume that this directory was created by SGI's "make", and (if
things hadn't gone nuts) would have been removed when it finished.

The fact that the there are two .I files seems consistent with the
fact that (as reported by the guy who was trying to build his job)
each "make" that hung seemed to cause two unkillable "be" processes
to run.  And the fact that there were six running this morning is
consistent with the fact that he started his "make" three times.

In any case, has anyone else encountered this problem, or anything
similar?  Are there any known bugs related to DUnix v3 NFS servers
and/or IRIX 6.4 NFS clients?  Patches?  Advice or suggestions?  As
usual, many thanks in advance.

