	Check the SGI web pages for the latest patches; there were a
large number of patches to IRIX through 5.3 before SGI's NFS became
stable when served to non-SGI platforms.

	To keep our SGIs happy, we button the Digital Unix boxes down
to NFS version 2.  As of 5.3, SGI had trouble with the 64bit pointers
from the DEC machines; this is probably not true of 6.4, but I haven't
tried it yet.

	Try to turn off the SGI feature that allows asynchronous
writes in NFS.  In my opinion, this is a time bomb waiting to happen.
We had several meltdowns wherein the SGI box was convinced it did not
know the route to a machine on its own subnet and flooded the router
with the packets, the router then sent the packets to the DEC and the
DEC answered the SGI request, which then cause the SGI to issue
another request to the router, which...  The machines stayed up
through that, but the performance was horrible.

	Finally, check your switch regularly to make sure that there
is not a malfunctioning ethernet adaptor in your domain that may be
hitting the SGI with runt packets.  Runts seem to cause a great deal
of trouble to our IRIX 5.3 systems.  Your Mileage May Vary in 6.4

	Please post the answers, as some of us also face the 6.4
upgrade.

	Thanks,

	Peter David

